ing participle clause to the **subject of the main clause**.","explanation":"The introductory participle clause normally shares its understood subject with **the assistant**, the main-clause subject.","keywords":["shared-participle-subject"],"skill":"shared-participle-subject","contextKey":"participle-clauses-3","fingerprint":"c4823608ea91b08a6a94606300f15fa25f331f9252589a3224518eb137af24f8","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-participles"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Link the opening participle clause to the **subject of the main clause**.","The introductory participle clause normally shares its understood subject with **the assistant**, the main-clause subject.","Choose **the assistant**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The main subject supplies the understood actor in this construction.","B":"The introductory participle clause normally shares its understood subject with **the assistant**, the main-clause subject.","C":"This is the object of the earlier action, not the actor.","D":"The structure normally links the implied actor to the main-clause subject."}}},{"id":"87f4a7d7-29e6-50fe-ac66-0c7b1c08d6d7","topicSlug":"participle-clauses","level":"C1","prompt":"Add an active participle clause: “____ the travel leaflet, the assistant noticed a mistake.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Has translated"},{"id":"B","text":"translating"},{"id":"C","text":"To translate"},{"id":"D","text":"translates"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"The assistant performs both actions, so an **active -ing clause** can share that subject.","explanation":"**translating the travel leaflet** describes the assistant’s activity; the main subject supplies the understood actor.","keywords":["active-participle"],"skill":"active-participle","contextKey":"participle-clauses-4","fingerprint":"e1e75084a7d6ab8a