e the **past participle** for an earlier completed action.","**Having prepared** marks the activity as completed before the break.","Choose **prepared**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"A to-infinitive does not follow having in a perfect participle clause.","B":"This would foreground earlier ongoing activity rather than the requested completed simple-perfect action.","C":"**Having prepared** marks the activity as completed before the break.","D":"Having requires a past participle for this perfect clause."}}},{"id":"3c28cb4c-3767-5241-ae65-49b1ee4a1bfb","topicSlug":"participle-clauses","level":"C1","prompt":"Reduce “who is doing the task”: “The assistant ____ the evening meal is new.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"has prepared"},{"id":"B","text":"to prepare"},{"id":"C","text":"preparing"},{"id":"D","text":"prepares"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"The noun is the **actor**, and the action is in progress: use an **-ing modifier**.","explanation":"**preparing the evening meal** is an active reduced relative modifying the assistant.","keywords":["reduced-active-relative"],"skill":"reduced-active-relative","contextKey":"participle-clauses-49","fingerprint":"e0bc6c521f3221979e2781f7c582c289b63196a80517c4b553db62b91e8bcf7b","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-participles"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The noun is the **actor**, and the action is in progress: use an **-ing modifier**.","**preparing the evening meal** is an active reduced relative modifying the assistant.","Choose **preparing**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"A finite perfect clause cannot follow the noun directly without a connector in this construction.","B":"An infinitive suggests a task to be done rather than the stated ongoing activity.","C":"**preparing the evening meal** is an active reduced relative modifying the assistant.","D":"A second finite verb here needs a relative pronoun or another clause structure."}}},{"id":"29eb1885-1af6-5e22-a9d8-4fa1434aabae","topicSlug":"participle-clauses","level":"C1","prompt":"In “Having prepared the evening meal, the assistant left”, who performed the earlier action?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"the assistant"},{"id":"B","text":"the evening meal"},{"id":"C","text":"an unnamed different person"},{"id":"D","text":"the sentence does not imply an actor"}],"answerId":"A","hint":"Link the opening participle clause to the **subject of the main clause**.","explanation":"The introductory participle clause normally shares its understood subject with **the assistant**, the main-clause subject.","keywords":["shared-participle-subject"],"skill":"shared-participle-subject","contextKey":"participle-clauses-49","fingerprint":"44e7e66f6acafe83013620250e889abd15ccf566020280490dd0a06d7b7d623b","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-participles"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Link the opening participle clause to the **subject of the main clause**.","The introductory participle clause normally shares its understood subject with **the assistant**, the main-clause subject.","Choose **the assistant**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The introductory participle clause normally shares its understood subject with **the assistant**, the main-clause subject.","B":"This is the object of the earlier action, not the actor.","C":"The structure normally links the implied actor to the main-clause subject.","D":"The main subject supplies the understood actor in this construction."}}},{"id":"cd419c97-333f-5ba9-a8a4-5df1ff1969ac","topicSlug":"participle-clauses","level":"C1","prompt":"Add an active participle clause: “____ the rowing boat, the assistant noticed a mistake.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"To repair"},{"id":"B","text":"repairs"},{"id":"C","text":"Has repaired"},{"id":"D","text":"repairing"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"The assistant performs both actions, so an **active -ing clause** can share that subject.","explanation":"**repairing the rowing boat** describes the assistant’s activity; the main subject supplies the understood actor.","keywords":["active-participle"],"skill":"active-participle","contextKey":"participle-clauses-50","fingerprint":"ad6f4f3f88f10d25aef5627cd203e6baf8f15297f33863780322f8b09ba028cf","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-participles"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The assistant performs both actions, so an **active -ing clause** can share that subject.","**repairing the rowing boat** describes the assistant’s activity; the main subject supplies the understood actor.","Choose **repairing**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"A to-infinitive would normally suggest purpose or a different relation, not the requested participle clause.","B":"A finite -s verb cannot open this subjectless participle clause.","C":"A finite perfect verb requires its own subject and is not this participle construction.","D":"**repairing the rowing boat** describes the assistant’s activity; the main subject supplies the understood actor."}}},{"id":"8c362530-7c71-5530-aa13-321c4bcc1137","topicSlug":"participle-clauses","level":"C1","prompt":"Make the earlier completion explicit: “Having ____ the rowing boat, we took a break.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"repairing"},{"id":"B","text":"to repair"},{"id":"C","text":"been repairing"},{"id":"D","text":"repaired"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"After **having**, choose the **past participle** for an earlier completed action.","explanation":"**Having repaired** marks the activity as completed before the break.","keywords":["perfect-participle"],"skill":"perfect-participle","contextKey":"participle-clauses-50","fingerprint":"c4c06efcb3814657de591c157a1260d4db9304374eda93248b99b9a3cb6d0353","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-participles"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["After **having**, choose the **past participle** for an earlier completed action.","**Having repaired** marks the activity as completed before the break.","Choose **repaired**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Having requires a past participle for this perfect clause.","B":"A to-infinitive does not follow having in a perfect participle clause.","C":"This would foreground earlier ongoing activity rather than the requested completed simple-perfect action.","D":"**Having repaired** marks the activity as completed before the break."}}},{"id":"194f5e39-4ee0-5ece-a23b-361a9e6c8495","topicSlug":"participle-clauses","level":"C1","prompt":"Reduce “who is doing the task”: “The assistant ____ the rowing boat is new.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"to repair"},{"id":"B","text":"repairing"},{"id":"C","text":"repairs"},{"id":"D","text":"has repaired"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"The noun is the **actor**, and the action is in progress: use an **-ing modifier**.","explanation":"**repairing the rowing boat** is an active reduced relative modifying the assistant.","keywords":["reduced-active-relative"],"skill":"reduced-active-relative","contextKey":"participle-clauses-50","fingerprint":"ee9a3ec8e8f3b39c93b4ab8fb1abafbb83eef3a509a3aaa49465bf259ae6dcc3","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-participles"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The noun is the **actor**, and the action is in progress: use an **-ing modifier**.","**repairing the rowing boat** is an active reduced relative modifying the assistant.","Choose **repairing**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"An infinitive suggests a task to be done rather than the stated ongoing activity.","B":"**repairing the rowing boat** is an active reduced relative modifying the assistant.","C":"A second finite verb here needs a relative pronoun or another clause structure.","D":"A finite perfect clause cannot follow the noun directly without a connector in this construction."}}},{"id":"49178d0c-bdfc-5bcc-a0e4-21fe900460cf","topicSlug":"participle-clauses","level":"C1","prompt":"In “Having repaired the rowing boat, the assistant left”, who performed the earlier action?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"the sentence does not imply an actor"},{"id":"B","text":"the assistant"},{"id":"C","text":"the rowing boat"},{"id":"D","text":"an unnamed different person"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"Link the opening participle clause to the **subject of the main clause**.","explanation":"The introductory participle clause normally shares its understood subject with **the assistant**, the main-clause subject.","keywords":["shared-participle-subject"],"skill":"shared-participle-subject","contextKey":"participle-clauses-50","fingerprint":"366acc424147de45f5b2d2d7c4df3b08e2ad5c159effaf7a8ff3cd047b310e83","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-participles"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Link the opening participle clause to the **subject of the main clause**.","The introductory participle clause normally shares its understood subject with **the assistant**, the main-clause subject.","Choose **the assistant**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The main subject supplies the understood actor in this construction.","B":"The introductory participle clause normally shares its understood subject with **the assistant**, the main-clause subject.","C":"This is the object of the earlier action, not the actor.","D":"The structure normally links the implied actor to the main-clause subject."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Make related information more compact while keeping the implied subject and time relationship clear.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"An active -ing clause","explanation":"A present-participle clause can add a reason, simultaneous activity or result. In an introductory clause, its understood subject normally matches the main-clause subject. Its form does not by itself set a present tense; the main clause and context provide time.","pattern":"**Walking home**, she called her brother.","commonMistakes":["Walking home, the rain began suggests that the rain was walking. This is a dangling modifier."],"examples":["Add an active participle clause: “**preparing** the visitor guide, the assistant noticed a mistake.”","Add an active participle clause: “**restoring** the old portrait, the assistant noticed a mistake.”"],"skill":"form"},{"title":"A passive participle clause","explanation":"A past-participle clause usually has a passive meaning: the implied subject receives the action. It can express a condition, reason or accompanying description. The main clause supplies the participant and time.","pattern":"**Kept cool**, the food lasts longer.","commonMistakes":["A past participle does not automatically mean past time; it often marks passive meaning."],"examples":["Make the earlier completion explicit: “Having **prepared** the visitor guide, we took a break.”","Make the earlier completion explicit: “Having **restored** the old portrait, we took a break.”"],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"A completed earlier action","explanation":"Use **having + past participle** to make completion before the main action explicit. The passive equivalent is having been + participle. This is useful when sequence might otherwise be unclear, but long chains of participles can become hard to read.","pattern":"**Having checked** the map, we left.","commonMistakes":["Having checking is not the perfect-participle form: having takes a past participle."],"examples":["Reduce “who is doing the task”: “The assistant **preparing** the visitor guide is new.”","Reduce “who is doing the task”: “The assistant **restoring** the old portrait is new.”"],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"Reduced relative clauses","explanation":"An -ing phrase after a noun can correspond to an active relative clause; a past-participle phrase can correspond to a passive relative. Keep the relation close to its noun and ensure that the reduction preserves the intended meaning.","pattern":"The person **waiting outside** is here. / The letter **sent yesterday** arrived.","commonMistakes":["Not every finite relative clause can be reduced without changing its meaning, tense or emphasis."],"examples":["In “Having prepared the visitor guide, the assistant left”, who performed the earlier action? → **the assistant**","In “Having restored the old portrait, the assistant left”, who performed the earlier action? → **the assistant**"],"skill":"application"}],"recap":["**Walking home**, she called her brother.","**Kept cool**, the food lasts longer.","**Having checked** the map, we left.","The person **waiting outside** is here. / The letter **sent yesterday** arrived."]},"stage":"6 · Advanced control","prerequisites":["reporting-verb-patterns"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
